          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

      <!-- Editorial draft banner -->
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4">
        <div class="not-prose my-8 bg-amber-50 border-2 border-amber-300 rounded-2xl p-6">
          <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-amber-800 mb-2">
            Editorial draft — not yet published
          </p>
          <p class="text-sm text-gray-800 leading-relaxed m-0 mb-3">
            The framing and analysis below are complete. The following need verified
            manufacturer data before this page goes live, and nothing on this page should be
            treated as factual until they are filled in:
          </p>
          <ul class="text-sm text-gray-800 leading-relaxed m-0 pl-5">
            {DATA_PENDING.map((item) => (
              <li>{item}</li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">September 15, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="2.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#who-its-for" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Who the Step Up Is Actually For</a>
          <a href="#capacity-and-frame" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. Capacity, Frame Mass and What They Really Tell You</a>
          <a href="#what-to-verify" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What to Confirm Before Buying</a>
          <a href="#console-and-subscription" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. Console, Screen and the Subscription Position</a>
          <a href="#against-commercial" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. When to Spend Past It Instead</a>
          <a href="#ownership" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Delivery, Placement and Keeping It Alive</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The F85 is the machine people buy when they want to be certain they have bought enough
          treadmill. That is a reasonable instinct and an expensive one, because the step up from
          the F80 is real but narrow: it buys headroom rather than a different kind of machine.
          Whether that headroom is worth several hundred dollars depends on facts about you, not
          about the treadmill.
        </p>

        <div id="who-its-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Who the Step Up Is Actually For</h2>
<p>
            Sole's folding range steps upward in a straight line, and by the time you reach the
            F85 the previous rungs have already solved most problems. The
            <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">F63</a> covers
            walking, the <a href="/sole-f65-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">F65</a>
            adds the deck length that makes running viable, and the
            <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">F80</a> adds motor
            and belt width on a frame that can use them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Which leaves the F85 answering a narrower question: what if the F80 is not quite
            enough? There are three buyers for whom it genuinely is not.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The heavier runner.</strong> Weight capacity is a limit on paper and a proxy
            for frame rigidity in practice. A runner near the F80's ceiling is operating at the
            edge of the envelope on every footfall, and the F85's additional capacity buys margin
            rather than permission.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The shared household.</strong> Two or three people using a machine daily is a
            different duty cycle from one person three times a week. Motors in this class do not
            fail from a hard session; they degrade from sustained heat, and headroom is what keeps
            the motor operating below the point where that starts.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>The high-mileage single user.</strong> Someone running most days, especially
            at pace or on sustained incline, is asking for the same thing the household is. If
            that is you, read <a href="/best-treadmills-for-running/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our running guide</a>
            first — deck length is still the specification that decides the most, and no amount of
            motor compensates for a deck that is short for your stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            If none of those describe you, the honest answer is that the F80 is the machine and
            the difference will show up on your statement rather than on your runs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="capacity-and-frame" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Capacity, Frame Mass and What They Really Tell You</h2>
<p>
            Weight capacity is the specification most often read as a safety limit and most
            usefully read as an engineering summary. A manufacturer arrives at it from frame
            construction, deck thickness, roller diameter and motor rating together, which is why
            it correlates with how planted a machine feels far better than horsepower does.
          </p>
          <p>
            The working rule we apply across the site: take your body weight, add 25%, and treat
            that as the minimum capacity worth buying. The margin is not about the scale — it is
            about impact. A running footfall puts roughly two and a half to three times body
            weight through the deck, thousands of times per session, and a frame operating close
            to its rated limit transmits that movement back into your stride.
          </p>
          <p>
            Assembled weight is the other half of the same story and the more honest number,
            because mass is what resists movement. This is the specification that most affects
            delivery, placement and whether the machine can ever be moved again, and it is the one
            buyers discover last. Confirm it before you decide which room the treadmill is going
            in, alongside the clearance figures in
            <a href="/treadmill-dimensions-space-requirements/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">dimensions and space requirements</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            For context on where this class sits, our
            <a href="/treadmills-over-2500/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">premium tier guide</a>
            covers what the next price band buys and why the improvements above it compound
            slowly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-to-verify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What to Confirm Before Buying</h2>
<p>
            The F85 shares the F80's documentation problem: several generations sold under one
            name, retailer listings carrying forward older specification blocks, and reviews that
            refresh their date rather than their measurements. The
            <a href="/sole-f80-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">F80 page</a>
            explains why that happens across this range. The practical response is the same list,
            answered against the exact model year you are being sold.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Continuous-duty horsepower for this model year.</strong> Not peak, and not the
            figure quoted on a review of the previous generation. This is the specification the
            step up from the F80 is supposedly built on, so it is the one worth being certain
            about — see the
            <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            for why the two numbers differ so widely.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Weight capacity, and assembled weight.</strong> Both, and in writing. Sources
            disagree on the first, and the second determines your delivery arrangements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Incline range, and whether decline is present.</strong> Decline has appeared on
            some units in this family and not others, and it is the feature most often assumed
            from a sibling model or an older review. If you are buying for downhill preparation,
            this question is the purchase.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Running surface, measured against your stride.</strong> Use the
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a>
            rather than the category average, and remember that usable deck is always shorter than
            the published belt figure.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Warranty, split five ways.</strong> Frame, motor, deck, parts and labour are
            separate terms. Labour is the one that decides what a fault costs in year four, and
            whether service is in-home or return-to-base decides what it costs you in
            inconvenience.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console behaviour with no account.</strong> Covered in the next section, and
            worth a direct answer rather than an inference from the brand's reputation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="console-and-subscription" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Console, Screen and the Subscription Position</h2>
<p>
            Sole's standing argument is that you are buying hardware rather than joining an
            ecosystem, and that is the main reason machines in this range are cross-shopped
            against NordicTrack and ProForm at all. The reasoning is straightforward: a lower
            purchase price attached to a recurring fee can cost more across five years than a
            higher price that asks for nothing, and that comparison is almost never presented at
            the point of sale.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Our subscription-free guide</a>
            works the arithmetic through. The part to verify on this specific machine is what its
            console does unattached, because "no subscription required" spans a wide range in
            practice. Ask three questions: can speed and incline be set manually without an
            account, do the built-in programmes run, and is workout history retained locally.
          </p>
          <p>
            Larger screens have appeared on machines in this family, and a screen is the component
            most likely to change between generations while everything structural stays the same.
            That is worth knowing in both directions — it means a newer model year may be a
            console update rather than a better treadmill, which makes the outgoing unit the best
            value in the range more often than not.
          </p>
          <p>
            It also affects what the machine is worth later. A treadmill that functions completely
            on its own holds value in the used market in a way a subscription-dependent console
            does not, which our <a href="/new-vs-used-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">new versus used</a>
            comparison sees from the buyer's side.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="against-commercial" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. When to Spend Past It Instead</h2>
<p>
            The F85 sits at the top of a folding consumer range, which means the next step is not
            a bigger version of the same thing — it is a different category of machine. Knowing
            where that boundary falls stops you buying the most expensive answer to the wrong
            question.
          </p>
          <p>
            The commercial machines — the
            <a href="/life-fitness-t3-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Life Fitness T3</a>
            and the <a href="/matrix-fitness-t7xe-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Matrix T7xe</a>
            among them — are built around AC motors and frames designed for eight hours of daily
            use. They do not fold, they need a permanent planned location, and they cost roughly
            double. What they buy is that nothing changes: deck feel at year five matches year
            one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The case for spending up is duty cycle and duration, not peak performance. If you are
            running 40 miles a week and intend to keep doing so for a decade, the arithmetic
            works. If you are running 15, it does not, and
            <a href="/commercial-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our commercial guide</a>
            says so plainly.
          </p>
          <p>
            The case against is simpler: folding is a real constraint on a commercial machine and
            a real feature on this one. If the treadmill shares a room with anything else, the
            F85 is the more useful machine at any mileage.
            <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">The folding roundup</a>
            covers where that trade-off lands across the category.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ownership" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Delivery, Placement and Keeping It Alive</h2>
<p>
            A machine at the top of this range arrives crated and heavy, and the delivery decision
            is a genuine part of the purchase rather than a formality. Kerbside is the default
            almost everywhere, in-room placement plus assembly is a separately priced service, and
            the difference is a flight of stairs and two people you may not have.
            <a href="/treadmills-for-sale/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Where to buy and what to pay</a>
            covers what each channel includes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Folding reclaims floor area and lets you clean under the deck. It does not make the
            machine portable: transport wheels are effective on hard floors and close to useless
            on carpet, and if the treadmill is going on carpet at all, read
            <a href="/treadmill-on-carpet/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills on carpet</a>
            first for what it does to motor compartment airflow.
          </p>
          <p>
            Then maintain it, because machines in this class are limited by maintenance rather
            than by manufacturing. Lubricate the deck on the manual's interval for its specific
            deck type — <a href="/treadmill-belt-lubrication/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the walkthrough</a>
            and <a href="/how-often-to-lubricate-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the interval question</a>
            cover both halves — and diagnose
            <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">slipping and tracking</a>
            as the separate faults they are rather than tightening the belt until something stops.
          </p>
          <p>
            <a href="/treadmill-maintenance/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">The full owner's schedule</a>
            takes about ten minutes a month, and
            <a href="/how-long-do-treadmills-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">our lifespan guide</a>
            covers what a maintained machine in this class should reasonably deliver and when a
            repair stops being worth paying for.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the Sole F85 worth it over the F80?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a heavier runner, a household sharing the machine daily, or someone running most days, yes — the step up buys motor headroom and capacity margin, which is what keeps a machine operating inside its envelope rather than at the edge of it. For a single user of average weight running a few times a week, the F80 is the machine and the difference will show up on your statement rather than on your runs.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Sole F85 have decline?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Decline has appeared on some units in this family and not on others, and it is the feature most often assumed from a sibling model or an older review. Confirm it against the manufacturer's page for the exact model year you are being sold. If you are buying specifically for downhill race preparation, that question is effectively the purchase decision.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What weight capacity does a treadmill need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Take your body weight, add 25%, and treat that as the minimum. The margin is about impact rather than the scale: a running footfall puts roughly two and a half to three times body weight through the deck thousands of times per session, and a frame working near its rated limit transmits that movement back into your stride.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Sole F85 require a subscription?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sole has historically sold treadmills that run fully without an account or a recurring fee, which is the main reason the range is cross-shopped against subscription-led brands. Verify it for the current console rather than the reputation: ask whether speed and incline can be set manually without an account, whether the built-in programmes run, and whether workout history is retained locally.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a Sole F85 or a commercial treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The boundary is duty cycle and duration, not peak performance. Commercial machines use AC motors and frames built for eight hours of daily use, do not fold, and cost roughly double — worth it at 40 miles a week sustained over years, not at 15. If the treadmill shares a room with anything else, folding is a real advantage the commercial machines cannot offer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do listings show different specs for the Sole F85?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Several generations have been sold under the same name with genuine changes underneath it, retailer specification blocks are copied forward when stock is replenished, and long-lived reviews update their dates rather than their measurements. The result is that old figures stay in circulation. Check the manufacturer's page for your model year before treating any number as current.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying at the Top of a Range</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The F85 is bought defensively more often than any other machine in the range, and defensive buying produces its own set of errors.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying headroom you will never use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The step up from the F80 buys motor margin and capacity margin. Margin is genuinely valuable to a heavy runner, a shared household or a daily high-mileage user, and is invisible to everyone else. Work out which of those you are before paying for it, because the same money spent on delivery, assembly and a proper mat improves the experience for every buyer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the newer model year is a better treadmill</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Annual refreshes in this category are overwhelmingly console and software changes over an unchanged frame, motor and deck. That makes the outgoing model year frequently the best value in the range. Ask the retailer explicitly what changed between years, and treat a vague answer as evidence that the answer is the screen.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading capacity as a limit rather than as a proxy</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Weight capacity summarises frame construction, deck thickness, roller diameter and motor rating together, which is why it predicts how planted a machine feels better than horsepower does. Buying to your exact weight rather than to weight plus 25% puts you at the edge of the envelope on every footfall, which is where machines feel unstable and wear quickly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning the room but not the delivery route</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">This is a crated freight item of substantial weight, delivered kerbside unless you pay for more. Doorway widths, stair turns and whether the crate can be opened outside all matter, and all of them are cheaper to think about before ordering than on the day. Confirm assembled weight and folded dimensions first, then decide what delivery service you are buying.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

